---

## 1. Fishbone Diagram (Ishikawa Diagram)

### What It Is
The Fishbone Diagram is a **cause-and-effect** diagram. The "fish head" (right side) represents the **main problem** your project addresses, and the "bones" represent the **categories of root causes** that contribute to that problem.

### The Main Problem (Effect)
Based on your Chapter 1 Project Context, the effect is:

> **"Low Math Engagement and Increased Mathematics Anxiety Among Grade 4–6 Students"**

This is directly from your project context — Richardson & Suinn (1972) defined math anxiety, and Ashcraft (2002) described how it occupies working memory [Ch.1 references 3, 4].

### The 6 Root Cause Categories

| Category | What It Covers | Your Project's Causes |
|----------|---------------|----------------------|
| **People** | Human factors — students, teachers | • Students lack motivation for repetitive drills<br>• Different students learn at different paces<br>• Math anxiety occupies working memory (Ashcraft, 2002) |
| **Methods** | Teaching/learning approaches | • "Chocolate-Covered Broccoli" effect — drills disguised as games<br>• Static difficulty curves — no adaptive scaling<br>• Traditional "drill-and-kill" classroom approach |
| **Technology** | Existing tools & platforms | • Existing games require constant internet (Prodigy, Grand Prix)<br>• Paid/freemium models create access barriers<br>• No offline-capable educational RPGs available |
| **Environment** | Classroom & setting conditions | • Classroom grading creates fear of failure<br>• No safe, low-stakes practice environment<br>• Timed academic assessments increase stress |
| **Content / Materials** | Learning materials & games | • Math drills are repetitive with no narrative context<br>• Limited replay value in existing math games<br>• Existing games cover only single operations (e.g., Grand Prix = multiplication only) |
| **Management** | Oversight, feedback, collaboration | • No real-time feedback on student performance<br>• No peer collaboration tools for cooperative learning<br>• Educators have limited control over learning progression |

### Where Each Cause Comes From in Your Chapters

| Cause | Source |
|-------|--------|
| Math anxiety & working memory | Ch.1 §1.1 — Ashcraft (2002) [4] |
| Chocolate-Covered Broccoli | Ch.1 §1.1 — paragraph 5 |
